---
description: Integration & automation patterns (trigger-driven, idempotent, auditable)
alwaysApply: true
---

Use this rule when the user asks for automation, approval flows, or cross-module integration.

PRINCIPLES
- Triggers must be explicit. Define WHAT event starts automation (Server Action, DB insert/update, schedule).
- Automation must be idempotent (safe to retry). Always define a dedupe key.
- Every automation needs an audit trail (who/what/when), retry policy, and failure visibility.

RPC FUNCTION PATTERN (MANDATORY FOR SYNCHRONIZATION LOGIC)
- **REQUIREMENT**: Logic sinkronisasi antar tabel/modul WAJIB menggunakan RPC function di PostgreSQL.
- Why RPC over multiple queries:
  - Type safety at PostgreSQL level
  - Single round trip, atomic transaction
  - All-or-nothing execution
  - Business logic centralized and auditable
- RPC Function Pattern:
  - Version in `supabase/migrations/**` with timestamp prefix (Rule 32)
  - Default `SECURITY INVOKER`; `SECURITY DEFINER` only with RLS review
  - Grant EXECUTE to `authenticated`; revoke from `public` when appropriate
  - End migration with `NOTIFY pgrst, 'reload schema';`

RECOMMENDED PATTERN (simple and robust)
1) Define domain event(s): `event_name`, `payload`, `source`, `dedupe_key`, `created_at`, `processed_at`, `status`, `error`.
2) Write to an `outbox_events` table inside the same transaction as the state change.
3) A worker/cron (Edge Function or pg_cron) processes events:
   - validates current state (guard)
   - performs side effects
   - marks event processed
   - retries on retryable errors; dead-letter on permanent failures.

WHAT TO DELIVER (when asked)
- A short state machine diagram (states + transitions)
- Trigger list (event name, when fired, payload)
- Idempotency strategy (dedupe key)
- Audit log fields
- Minimal DB + worker changes required
